# Phase 004 temporal evidence rules

Status: **Groups 01–03 accepted — REF-001–REF-020. Group 04 next and not started.**

Preserve:

- event/effective time ≠ source production time ≠ source availability time ≠ framework collection time ≠ framework recorded/knowledge time ≠ derived evaluation time;
- source availability ≠ framework knowledge;
- framework knowledge ≠ actual historical interpretation/communication;
- current retrieval of an old fact ≠ historical framework knowledge;
- `known by K` ≠ `learned after K` ≠ `not recorded by K` ≠ `not known by K` ≠ `not available by K`;
- late evidence ≠ source correction ≠ independent conflict ≠ reinterpretation ≠ later authority resolution;
- actual retained historical state ≠ replay-derived reconstruction;
- early operational validation ≠ enriched health ≠ RCA ≠ post-operations retrospective review.

Rules:

- For an `as-known` cut at event/window `T` and knowledge cutoff `K`, include only evidence applicable to `T` whose framework recorded/knowledge time is at or before `K`.
- Corrections/supersessions used in the cut must themselves have been known by `K`.
- A source record that existed/queryable before `K` but was not collected by the framework is not silently treated as framework-known by `K`.
- A current query of historical source state produces current framework knowledge unless retained evidence proves earlier knowledge.
- `Not known by K` requires sufficient opportunity-to-observe/retention coverage; missing history or monitoring outage cannot establish the negative.
- Produce the narrowest trustworthy result as soon as its evidence standard is satisfied; do not wait for slower enrichment merely for completeness.
- A fast `job succeeded` result never implies freshness/quality/pipeline health while those evidence classes are pending.
- Do not weaken gate/safeguard/causal or other high-consequence evidence standards for latency convenience.
- Preserve the analytical horizons: immediate operational validation, enriched health evaluation, investigative/RCA reasoning, retrospective/post-ops review.
- Causal maturity can further progress from proposed/candidate through supported/weakened/unresolved evaluation to deeper RCA and confirmation review; timing itself does not upgrade causal status.
- Do not treat those horizons as services, workflows, or fixed SLAs.
- Late/corrected evidence creates later knowledge/evaluation state and never backdates contemporaneous knowledge.
- Explicit source correction preserves prior state; independent disagreement remains conflict unless an accepted authority/correction rule resolves it.
- Reevaluate retained conclusions only where new evidence materially bears on their basis/applicability/coverage/contradiction set.
- Closed Investigations can become review/reopen candidates when materially challenged; do not auto-reopen every closed Investigation.
- Call historical state `actual` only when provenance-bearing evidence establishes that state/action/communication existed then. Otherwise label it replay-derived/reconstructed.
- Historical causal confirmation remains actual historical state when provenance establishes it existed then, even if later evidence changes the current claim status.
- Current requester authorization governs disclosure of historical and reconstructed state.

Timing handoff:

- Phase 006 defines health-result availability/freshness objectives by analytical horizon.
- Phase 008 defines communication of progressive health/RCA maturity.
- Phase 009 characterizes source evidence availability/collection latency and query cost/retention.
- Phase 010 selects fast-path/asynchronous architecture and performance budgets while preserving non-blocking passive monitoring.
- Phase 011 turns accepted timing objectives into MVP acceptance criteria.

Do not begin Phase 004 Group 04 without explicit user request.
